.TH "RECORD\-CLEAN\.SH" "" "March 2026" ""
.SH "NAME"
\fBrecord\-clean\.sh\fR
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBrecord\-clean\.sh\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBrecord\-clean\.sh\fR restores a RECORD experiment directory to a clean state after execution\.
.P
The command removes generated output files and invokes additional cleanup logic provided by the RECORD workflow library\.
.P
Before performing any cleanup, the script checks whether the RECORD run process is still active using the \fBpid_of_record_run\fR variable\. If the process is still running, the script reports the condition and exits without making changes\.
.P
If no active run process is detected, the script removes all files in the current directory matching the pattern \fB*\.out\fR\. It then executes the cleanup procedures defined in \fBlib\.record\.clean\.sh\fR\.
.P
This command is typically used to reset the experiment directory to the state it was in immediately after initialisation\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
This command takes no command\-line options\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Returns 0 on success\.
.P
Returns non\-zero if a RECORD run process is still active or if cleanup operations fail\.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
The script depends on variables and functions defined in the following RECORD workflow libraries:
.TP
\fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR
provides shared configuration variables including \fBpid_of_record_run\fR and \fBRECORD_START_PROGRAM\fR\.
.TP
\fBlib\.record\.clean\.sh\fR
provides additional cleanup logic for removing generated files and restoring the experiment directory state\.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
None\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Clean the experiment directory after a run:
.IP "" 4
.nf
record\-clean\.sh
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Typical workflow:
.IP "" 4
.nf
